Application High Availability with Kubernetes
Objectives
Describe how Kubernetes works to keep applications running after failures.

1. What is High Availability (HA) in Kubernetes?
High Availability (HA) means designing your application so it continues to run despite failures. Failures could be:
•	Application-level: bugs, crashes.
•	Environment-level: network outages, node failures.
•	Resource-level: other apps consuming all CPU/memory.
HA also protects the cluster from misbehaving applications (e.g., memory leaks).
Key point: HA does not fix bugs, but it ensures your app is resilient while bugs exist.
________________________________________
2. Application Requirements for HA
Kubernetes and OpenShift expect your applications to:
1.	Tolerate restarts – Pods can be killed and restarted anytime.
2.	Respond to health probes – Startup, readiness, and liveness probes help the cluster detect unhealthy pods.
3.	Support multiple instances – So traffic can continue if one instance fails.
4.	Manage resources properly – CPU and memory limits prevent crashes due to overuse.
5.	Operate with restricted privileges – For security and reliability.
Example: A web app with a /healthz endpoint returns 200 only if the database is reachable. Kubernetes can probe this and take action if unhealthy.
________________________________________
3. Kubernetes HA Mechanisms
Kubernetes uses several mechanisms to make applications highly available:
a) Restarting Pods
Pods can fail for many reasons. The restartPolicy field controls pod restart behavior:
Policy	Description
Always	Pod restarts regardless of exit reason (default for Deployments).
OnFailure	Pod restarts only if it exits with a non-zero code.
Never	Pod never restarts automatically.
Command Example: You can see pod restart counts:
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Tip: Deployments or StatefulSets usually handle pod restarts automatically.
________________________________________
b) Health Probes (Probing)
Kubernetes can detect unhealthy applications using probes:
1.	Liveness Probe – Checks if a pod is alive. If it fails, pod restarts.
2.	Readiness Probe – Checks if a pod is ready to serve traffic. If it fails, traffic is stopped but pod continues.
3.	Startup Probe – Checks if the application starts properly. Useful for slow-starting apps.
Example: Liveness probe in YAML:
livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 10
________________________________________
c) Horizontal Scaling
Kubernetes can scale pods horizontally to handle more traffic:
•	Deployment replicas: replicas define the number of pod instances.
•	Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA): Automatically scales pods based on CPU/memory or custom metrics.
Command Example:
# Scale a deployment manually
kubectl scale deployment myapp --replicas=3

# Create an autoscaler
kubectl autoscale deployment myapp --cpu-percent=50 --min=2 --max=5
________________________________________
4. Summary Flow of HA
1.	Application pod crashes → RestartPolicy / Deployment controller restarts pod.
2.	Pod running but unhealthy → Liveness/readiness probe detects and acts.
3.	Traffic increases → HPA or replica scaling ensures more pods handle load.
4.	Resource mismanagement → Resource limits prevent node from being overwhelmed.
________________________________________
5. Commands Recap
•	View pods & restarts: kubectl get pods / kubectl describe pod <name>
•	Scale deployment: kubectl scale deployment <name> --replicas=N
•	Autoscale deployment: kubectl autoscale deployment <name> --cpu-percent=50 --min=2 --max=5
•	Check pod logs: kubectl logs <pod-name>
________________________________________
✅ Key Takeaways:
•	HA is about resilience, not fixing bugs.
•	Use probes, replicas, and restart policies for reliability.
•	Applications must work with the cluster, not against it.
•	Controllers like Deployment and StatefulSet handle HA automatically.

